proposal.text:
{
  "annotator_prompt": [
    {
      "name": "right_target_center",
      "description": "After the instructed cube was lifted, the right target marking could be obscured although the visit count correctly required the first right visit. The policy guessed a different tabletop location. The existing memory therefore failed SUFFICIENCY. Add a field holding the fixed right target center as [x, y] in world metres. Initialize it from the first cycle in which the circular marking is clearly localizable using calibrated images and the tabletop plane; in these recordings this is the initial cycle. Retain that value thereafter, including while the marking is occluded. Use null before any such observation. Annotate from the visible marking, not from a later commanded waypoint or the actual arrival TCP. Example: [-0.0761, -0.1789].",
      "value_type": "world_coordinate"
    },
    {
      "name": "button_center",
      "description": "After the final cube release and retreat, the button could be obscured by the arm. The completed visit count remained correct, but the policy guessed a remote approach location because it had not retained the previously visible button location. This breaks SUFFICIENCY. Add a field holding the fixed button center as [x, y] in world metres. Initialize it at the first cycle with a clearly localizable button cap or base, which is the initial cycle in these recordings, and retain it thereafter. Use null before such an observation. Obtain the center from calibrated visible geometry at its surface height, not from the subsequent recorded action or the tracking-error-displaced TCP. Example: [-0.1759, 0.1970].",
      "value_type": "world_coordinate"
    }
  ],
  "policy_prompt_for_memory": [
    {
      "name": "right_target_center",
      "description": "A lifted cube and hand can hide the right marking just when target_visits requires moving there; counting visits alone then fails SUFFICIENCY. When this field is empty and the right circular target is clearly visible in the current cycle, locate the center of its concentric marking and back-project it onto the tabletop using the supplied calibration. Write its world [x, y], for example [-0.0761, -0.1789]. Right is the smaller-world-y target. Exclude nearby cubes when fitting the marking. Until localization is possible write null; once initialized retain the value, especially through occlusion. Do not replace it with the TCP position at an arrival."
    },
    {
      "name": "button_center",
      "description": "The button may disappear behind the arm after the cube is released, leaving the completed visit count insufficient to select the button approach. To restore SUFFICIENCY, initialize this field whenever it is empty and the current images clearly show the button's circular cap or square base. Store its calibrated world center [x, y], for example [-0.1759, 0.1970], and retain it thereafter. For the cap center use its raised top plane, approximately z=0.03 m; do not project it onto the tabletop or the commanded press height. A base-based estimate must use the base's own surface plane. Write null until a reliable observation exists. Never substitute the current hand position for the button center."
    }
  ],
  "policy_prompt_for_action": "The alternating-visit count and its observed-arrival transitions were correct; leave those rules unchanged. Failures occurred when the required destination was hidden, and when an approximately aligned hand or a feature projected at the wrong height was mistaken for the destination center. These are SUFFICIENCY failures of the spatial action rules, not reasons to add a task-phase counter.\n\nFor a right-target visit, use right_target_center when the current marking is obscured rather than guessing a location on blank tabletop. Continue to command z=0.10 m with the grasp maintained. For either target that is visible, localize the center of the concentric circular marking, excluding adjacent cubes; project the marking onto the tabletop, not onto the carried cube's plane. A neighboring colored cube must not shift the estimated target center.\n\nAt the pickup hover, replace any interpretation that descent preserves the reported hover TCP x,y with explicit visual recentering: back-project the center of the cube's top face onto approximately z=0.04 m, then command that horizontal center at TCP z=0.02 m and close. The top-face projection height and grasp TCP height are different. Ordinary hover tracking error is not evidence that the hand is centered.\n\nFor the button approach after release and clearance, use button_center if the button is hidden. If visible, localize the circular cap center with its approximately z=0.03 m raised top plane and the current camera calibration. Do not project the cap onto the tabletop, the approach plane, or the commanded press depth. Command the resulting button x,y at z=0.15 m, downward orientation and yaw zero, with the hand open.\n\nFor closing the empty gripper above the button, explicitly command the button's nominal x,y and z=0.15 m while closing, correcting lateral tracking error in that same action. Apply the 5 mm upward fallback only if this full nominal positional waypoint is within 2 mm of the reported TCP; do not use the fallback instead of correcting x,y. Before pressing, likewise use the visible calibrated button center, or its retained center if obscured, at commanded z=0.0075 m with the gripper closed. Being approximately above the button does not authorize copying current TCP x,y. Keep the existing contact-triggered retraction rule, retracting to the button center at z=0.15 m rather than repeatedly descending.",
  "refused": "The request to retain both target centers is only partly supported. The right marking became unavailable when its location was needed, so its center is added. The reported left-target miss occurred with the marking still visible beside another cube; that requires precise localization, not additional historical memory. Adding a left-target-center field for these failures would violate NECESSITY. No episode-specific waypoint constants are added to the action rules: the locations vary across scenes and must come from calibrated observations."
}

optimizer.text:
{
  "annotator_prompt": [
    {
      "kind": "add",
      "field": "right_target_center",
      "description": "Fixed center of the robot-right circular target, stored as [x, y] in world metres. Use null until the first cycle whose calibrated images clearly localize the concentric tabletop marking. At that cycle, record its center projected onto the tabletop plane, excluding neighboring cubes. Right is the smaller-world-y target. Retain this value thereafter, including through occlusion. Do not derive it from future frames, commanded waypoints, or arrival TCP positions. e.g. [-0.08, -0.18]",
      "value_type": "world_coordinate"
    },
    {
      "kind": "add",
      "field": "button_center",
      "description": "Fixed button center, stored as [x, y] in world metres. Use null until the first cycle whose calibrated images clearly localize the circular cap or square base. At that cycle, record the horizontal center using the observed feature's surface plane: approximately z=0.03 m for the cap, or the base's own surface height for the base. Retain this value thereafter, including through occlusion. Do not derive it from future frames, commanded waypoints, or TCP positions. e.g. [-0.18, 0.20]",
      "value_type": "world_coordinate"
    }
  ],
  "policy_prompt_for_memory": [
    {
      "kind": "add",
      "field": "right_target_center",
      "description": "Initialize to null. While null, inspect the current cycle's calibrated images for the robot-right target, the smaller-world-y target. When its concentric circular marking is clearly localizable, exclude neighboring cubes and project the marking's center onto the tabletop plane; store world [x, y] in metres. Otherwise keep null. Once initialized, retain the value unchanged, including through occlusion; never replace it with a commanded waypoint or arrival TCP position. e.g. [-0.08, -0.18]"
    },
    {
      "kind": "add",
      "field": "button_center",
      "description": "Initialize to null. While null, inspect the current cycle's calibrated images for a clearly localizable button cap or square base. Project the feature's center using its own surface plane, approximately z=0.03 m for the cap or the base's own surface height for the base, and store world [x, y] in metres. Otherwise keep null. Once initialized, retain the value unchanged, including through occlusion; never substitute a commanded waypoint or the hand position. e.g. [-0.18, 0.20]"
    }
  ],
  "policy_prompt_for_action": [
    {
      "modify_from": "Target centers lie essentially on the tabletop; account for the cube's height when estimating its center and for the button's raised surface when localizing it. Do not reuse coordinates from another episode.",
      "modify_to": "For either visible target, localize the center of its concentric circular marking, excluding neighboring cubes, and project onto the tabletop plane, not the carried cube's plane. If the right marking is obscured, use right_target_center from memory rather than guessing from blank tabletop. Localize the cube using its top-face plane, approximately z=0.04 m. For a visible button, project the circular cap center using its raised top plane, approximately z=0.03 m; a square-base estimate must use the base's own surface plane. Never project the cap onto the tabletop, approach plane, or press depth. If the button is obscured, use button_center from memory. Use newly observed centers when initializing these fields in the current cycle; a null field supplies no location. Do not reuse coordinates from another episode."
    },
    {
      "modify_from": "If already above it at that height, descend to its center at z=0.02 m and close.",
      "modify_to": "If already above it at that height, visually recenter before descending: back-project the cube's top-face center onto approximately z=0.04 m, then command that horizontal center at TCP z=0.02 m and close. The projection plane and grasp TCP height are different. Do not copy the reported hover TCP x,y merely because it is approximately aligned; correct ordinary hover tracking error in the descent."
    },
    {
      "modify_from": "When already above the button with an open hand, close the empty gripper at the button's nominal approach position. Ensure at least 2 mm of positional motion; if that nominal position is too close to the reported TCP, add a 5 mm upward displacement.",
      "modify_to": "When already above the button with an open hand, close the empty gripper while commanding the localized button center x,y at z=0.15 m, correcting lateral tracking error in that same action. Use the calibrated visible center, or button_center if obscured. Ensure at least 2 mm of positional motion: only if the full nominal positional waypoint is within 2 mm of the reported TCP, command the same button x,y at z=0.155 m instead. Do not use the upward fallback instead of correcting x,y."
    },
    {
      "modify_from": "When above the button with the empty gripper closed, descend at the button center to commanded z=0.0075 m, retaining the closed gripper and downward orientation.",
      "modify_to": "When above the button with the empty gripper closed, descend at the calibrated visible button center, or button_center if obscured, to commanded z=0.0075 m, retaining the closed gripper and downward orientation. Being approximately above the button does not authorize copying current TCP x,y."
    },
    {
      "modify_from": "Physical button contact may stop the actual TCP around z=0.024–0.025 m; when the cycle shows this low contact after the descent, retract above the same button to z=0.15 m with the gripper closed, rather than repeatedly pressing downward.",
      "modify_to": "Physical button contact may stop the actual TCP around z=0.024–0.025 m; when the cycle shows this low contact after the descent, retract to the localized button center x,y at z=0.15 m with the gripper closed, rather than repeatedly pressing downward. Use the calibrated visible center, or button_center if obscured, rather than the contact-displaced TCP x,y."
    }
  ],
  "refused": "No left-target-center field or task-phase counter is added: the reported left-target error occurred with the marking visible and is addressed by feature-specific localization, while visit-count transitions were correct. Episode-specific destination coordinates are not committed; all destinations come from calibrated observations. No earlier edits are undone."
}
